% assembly.weft — Output Routing
%
% This file contains all output directives for kcc.  It is the single
% point that maps logical code fragments to physical assembly files.
%
% When adding a new .s file to kcc, add its output directive HERE.
% Fragment definitions go in the appropriate chapter .weft file.

\chapter*{The Assembly of the Program}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{The Assembly of the Program}

Throughout this book each idea is told as its own story, and its code is
introduced as a \emph{named fragment} wherever the narrative is clearest---the
lexer's fragments in the lexer chapter, the register allocator's in the backend,
and so on, in the order that best serves a reader rather than a machine.  This
chapter is where those scattered fragments are finally \emph{assembled}: it is
the single place that says, for each physical file the computer requires, which
fragments compose it and in what order.

The word ``assembly'' carries both of its meanings here.  These are the
\emph{assembly-language} files---one \texttt{.s} per module---that the system
assembler turns into object code; and this chapter is the \emph{assembling} of
the program from its parts, the bill of materials that gathers a book's worth of
prose-embedded fragments into the concrete artifacts a build produces.  Reading
a single entry below tells you exactly what a given output file is made of: its
components, listed by name, resolved (through the fragment definitions in the
chapters) down to the last instruction.

Because a literate program separates \emph{where a fragment is explained} from
\emph{where it is emitted}, this is the only file in \kcc{} that uses output
directives (\texttt{@@o}); every other file merely \emph{defines} fragments
(\texttt{@@d}).  That separation is what lets the narrative flow by concept while
the build still receives well-formed, correctly ordered assembly files.  It also
makes the manifest a practical checklist: to add a new module to the compiler,
add its output directive here and its fragments to the appropriate chapter---the
two never drift, because the file the machine consumes is generated from exactly
these lists.

The directives below route code fragments to their physical assembly files under
\texttt{tmp/src/}.

\bigskip

